---
import ChronicleLayout from '../../layouts/ChronicleLayout.astro';
import ArticleHeader from '../../components/chronicle/ArticleHeader.astro';
import AnchorNav from '../../components/chronicle/AnchorNav.astro';
import Chapter from '../../components/chronicle/Chapter.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/chronicle/Figure.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../../components/chronicle/SectionHeading.astro';
import AtAGlance from '../../components/chronicle/AtAGlance.astro';
import FurtherReading from '../../components/chronicle/FurtherReading.astro';
import type { ChronicleMeta } from '../../components/chronicle/types';
import { SIZES_MODERN_WIDE } from '../../lib/responsive-image-config.mjs';

const meta: ChronicleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Things to Do in Santiago, Chile: A First-Hand Guide',
  headline: 'Best Things to Do in Santiago',
  description:
    'A first-hand guide to the best things to do in Santiago, Chile, from the historic centre and Vega Central to museums, parks and the city’s viewpoints.',
  path: '/best-things-to-do-in-santiago/',
  ogImage: '/images/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1200w.jpg',
  photography: true,
};

const IMG = '/images/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary';
---

<ChronicleLayout meta={meta}>
  <ArticleHeader
    provenance={meta}
    route={['Chile', 'Santiago', 'South America']}
    label="Guide · Six Experiences"
    title="Best Things to Do in Santiago"
    standfirst="Santiago kept changing register: formal civic buildings became working markets, museums opened into parks, neighbourhoods led to city-memory spaces, and the hills turned the whole route into geography. These are the experiences from my September 2026 visit that deserve priority on a first trip."
    stats={[
      { num: '6', label: 'Experiences' },
      { num: '4', label: 'Nights' },
      { num: '3', label: 'Viewpoints' },
      { num: '1', label: 'City' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <div class="opening-prose">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="drop-cap">
          Santiago first appeared as a formal centre: La Moneda, the Metropolitan
          Cathedral, broad streets and the old Mapocho edge. Then the scale shifted.
          Vega Central replaced civic architecture with stalls and produce; museums
          and Parque Forestal opened another layer; Yungay and the Sound Museum took
          the route away from the monumental centre; the hills and towers finally made
          the city legible from above.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a discovery guide, not another four-day itinerary. The six
          recommendations below come from the route recorded during my stay from 19
          to 23 September 2026. They are grouped by the experience they add, rather
          than by day. For the chronology, use the <a href="/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/">four-day Santiago itinerary</a>;
          for the detailed viewpoint comparison, use <a href="/santiago-viewpoints/">Santiago Viewpoints</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          I have kept out the unresolved Valparaíso note, the Bahá’í Temple, generic
          food recommendations, completed running claims and any current fares,
          opening hours or queue advice. Cementerio General remains a recorded
          supporting stop, but the evidence does not preserve enough first-hand detail
          to make it one of the headline recommendations here.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Santiago skyline with the mountains beyond the city."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Santiago becomes a different city each time the route changes register."
      priority
    />

    <div class="narrative prose">
      <SectionHeading id="how-to-use" rule={false}>How to use this list</SectionHeading>
      <p>
        The order moves from the central city towards the experiences that widened the
        route. It is not a universal ranking. Start with the historic centre and Vega
        Central if you want Santiago at street level; choose the museum and park route
        for culture; choose the viewpoint section when the city’s geography matters
        most.
      </p>
      <p>
        All route times and access limits below come from the travel record. They are
        not current opening hours, fares, queue estimates or transport instructions.
        The supplied Santiago archive contains 80 photographs but no GPS metadata, so
        captions stay with subjects that are visually or source-supported.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AnchorNav
      links={[
        { id: 'historic-centre', label: 'Historic centre' },
        { id: 'cathedral', label: 'Cathedral' },
        { id: 'vega', label: 'Vega Central' },
        { id: 'museums', label: 'Museums' },
        { id: 'park-route', label: 'Parks and Yungay' },
        { id: 'viewpoints', label: 'Viewpoints' },
        { id: 'priorities', label: 'Priorities' },
      ]}
    />
  </div>

  <AtAGlance
    id="glance"
    title="The Six Experiences at a Glance"
    meta={[
      {
        label: 'Start here',
        value: 'La Moneda, the Metropolitan Cathedral and the central streets form the clearest introduction to Santiago at street level.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Change the scale',
        value: 'Vega Central, the museums, Parque Forestal and the Yungay route move the visit beyond civic architecture.',
      },
      {
        label: 'Look from above',
        value: 'San Cristóbal Hill, Santa Lucía Hill and Sky Costanera are three recorded perspectives, not one repeated attraction.',
      },
    ]}
    daysHeading="Choose by interest"
    days={[
      { num: 'Civic', text: 'La Moneda · Metropolitan Cathedral · central streets · Estación Mapocho' },
      { num: 'Market', text: 'Vega Central · stalls · produce · working-market movement' },
      { num: 'Culture', text: 'Pre-Columbian Art · Fine Arts Museum · Sound Museum · Parque Forestal' },
      { num: 'Above', text: 'San Cristóbal · Santa Lucía · Sky Costanera · broad city geography' },
    ]}
  />

  <div class="post-body">
    <Chapter id="historic-centre" number="I." name="Explore Santiago’s historic centre" epithet="La Moneda, central streets, Estación Mapocho and the first civic layer" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The historic centre is the strongest first recommendation because it gives the
        city an initial scale. The recorded route includes the La Moneda Cultural
        Center, the Metropolitan Cathedral, central streets and Estación Mapocho. The
        spaces between those names matter as much as the buildings: the visit moved
        through institutions, public squares and the older transport-and-market edge
        rather than stopping at one landmark.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have kept La Moneda at the level the record supports. The stop is confirmed,
        but the archive does not establish a specific exhibition, interior visit or
        tour. The recommendation is the civic route itself, not a promise of access to
        a particular building.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-central-plaza.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-central-plaza-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-central-plaza-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-central-plaza-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-central-plaza.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A central Santiago plaza framed by civic buildings and a monument."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A central Santiago plaza from the civic route; the image is kept broad rather than assigned to one exact square."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="cathedral" number="II." name="Visit the Metropolitan Cathedral" epithet="A confirmed central landmark, kept separate from unsupported interior detail" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The Metropolitan Cathedral deserves its own place in the shortlist because the
        identity is secure in both the route and the processed photograph. It was part
        of the first central Santiago sequence, where the city’s civic and religious
        buildings stood close together.
      </p>
      <p>
        The evidence supports the Cathedral as a stop and the exterior as a confidently
        identified image. It does not preserve a service, a specific chapel, an
        interior tour or a detailed account of entering. That is enough for a useful
        recommendation: place the Cathedral inside the historic-centre route, without
        inventing a religious or architectural experience the archive does not record.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-metropolitan-cathedral.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-metropolitan-cathedral-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-metropolitan-cathedral-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-metropolitan-cathedral-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-metropolitan-cathedral.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The ornate facade of Santiago’s Metropolitan Cathedral."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="Santiago’s Metropolitan Cathedral, identified by its facade and twin towers."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="vega" number="III." name="Experience Vega Central" epithet="The route’s clearest shift from formal centre to working market" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Vega Central changes the city’s scale. The itinerary places it on the second
        recorded day, before the route returns to museums and Parque Forestal. The
        supported first-hand material is specific enough to say what made it different:
        stalls, produce and movement rather than civic facades and formal institutions.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is a market recommendation, not a food guide. I do not add a purchase, a
        dish, a price, a vendor conversation or opening-hour advice because none is
        preserved in the evidence. The useful reason to choose Vega is the change in
        register: it places the working-market city beside the museums and parks of
        the same wider route.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-park-street.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-park-street-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-street-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-street-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-street.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A tree-lined urban street setting in Santiago; exact location is unresolved."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A Santiago street sequence from the wider market, park and cultural route; exact location unresolved."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="museums" number="IV." name="Explore Santiago’s museums" epithet="Pre-Columbian Art, Fine Arts and the Sound Museum, held together by the route" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Santiago’s museum route is stronger as a group than as three thin entries. The
        itinerary records the Chilean Museum of Pre-Columbian Art, the National Museum
        of Fine Arts and the Sound Museum. They appear across the central, park and
        northern routes, adding collections and interiors to a visit otherwise easy to
        describe only through streets and viewpoints.
      </p>
      <p>
        The museum interior photograph is deliberately unnamed. It supports a museum
        sequence, not a specific gallery or collection. I would choose the museums by
        the shape of the surrounding route and by the collection you most want to see,
        then verify current access independently. The first-hand record does not carry
        a gallery-by-gallery review or present-day visitor logistics.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-museum-interior.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-museum-interior-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-museum-interior-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-museum-interior-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-museum-interior.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A wood-panelled museum interior in Santiago; exact museum is unresolved."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A museum interior from the Santiago route; the exact institution is not assigned."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="park-route" number="V." name="Walk the park and neighbourhood route" epithet="Parque Forestal, Yungay and the less monumental side of Santiago" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Parque Forestal gives the museum route room to breathe. It is recorded between
        the Pre-Columbian and Fine Arts stops, and works here as part of the cultural
        core rather than as a detailed park guide. The processed fountain image is
        confidently a Santiago park subject, but it is not relabelled as Parque
        Forestal or Santa Lucía from appearance alone.
      </p>
      <p>
        Yungay appears later in the route beside Cementerio General and the Sound
        Museum. I would include it as a cautious extension for travellers who want to
        see the route change away from the formal centre, not as a generic barrio
        profile. The evidence confirms the destination and its place in the route; it
        does not support broad claims about atmosphere, safety, cafés or local life.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cementerio General remains a supporting stop. The record names it, but the
        archive does not preserve enough internal exploration or cemetery-specific
        observation to promote it into one of the hub’s headline experiences.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-park-fountain.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-park-fountain-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-fountain-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-fountain-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-fountain.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="An ornate fountain and pavilion in a Santiago park; exact park is unresolved."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A park sequence from Santiago’s museum and neighbourhood route; exact park unresolved."
      variant="portrait"
    />
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-bolsa-de-comercio.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-bolsa-de-comercio-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-bolsa-de-comercio-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-bolsa-de-comercio-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-bolsa-de-comercio.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="The historic Bolsa de Comercio building in central Santiago."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="The Bolsa de Comercio, one of the identifiable historic façades in central Santiago."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <Chapter id="viewpoints" number="VI." name="See Santiago from above" epithet="Three recorded viewpoints, three different ways to read the city" />
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        The route included San Cristóbal Hill, Santa Lucía Hill and Sky Costanera.
        They belong in the hub because the city changes when seen from above, but the
        three should not be flattened into one repeated “panorama” recommendation.
      </p>
      <p>
        San Cristóbal is the hill-and-landscape option in the recorded comparison.
        Santa Lucía keeps the viewpoint tied to the central city and its park-like
        spaces. Sky Costanera supplies the built, high urban perspective. The specialist
        <a href="/santiago-viewpoints/">Santiago viewpoints guide</a> compares those three
        subjects in more detail; this page only answers whether the viewpoint layer
        deserves a place in a first visit.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Bahá’í Temple is not included. It appears in the wider itinerary record,
        but the available viewpoint evidence does not support promoting it into this
        comparison. Likewise, the route does not establish which transport system was
        used, current fares, exact opening arrangements or the viewpoint of every image.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop-1600w.jpg 1600w, ${IMG}/santiago-skyline-mountain-backdrop.jpg 2048w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="Santiago viewed from above with the mountains beyond the city; exact viewpoint is unresolved."
      width={2048}
      height={1365}
      caption="Santiago from above; the archive does not assign this broad panorama to one viewpoint."
    />

    <SectionHeading id="priorities">What I Would Prioritise</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        <strong>With one day:</strong> I would stay central: La Moneda, the
        Metropolitan Cathedral, the central streets and Vega Central, with one museum
        if access and interest allow. This gives the day civic, working-market and
        cultural registers without pretending that all of Santiago fits into one route.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With two days:</strong> add the museum-and-Parque Forestal route, then
        choose the viewpoint layer that best matches the kind of city view you want.
        The recorded evidence supports all three viewpoints, but it does not supply a
        universal order or current timetable.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>With four days:</strong> use the <a href="/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/">complete Santiago itinerary</a>
        for chronology, including the recorded Cementerio General, Yungay, Sound
        Museum and departure to Mendoza. This hub is the selection layer above that
        route, not a replacement for it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <SectionHeading id="surprised">What changed the way I saw Santiago</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Santiago became more interesting as the route moved away from one idea of the
        city. The formal centre established one register, Vega Central disrupted it,
        museums and Parque Forestal softened it, and Yungay and the Sound Museum made
        the route less monumental. The viewpoints then turned those separate places
        into a connected urban field.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the reason for this shortlist. The strongest first visit is not a race
        between famous names. It is a choice between different versions of Santiago:
        civic, commercial, cultural, neighbourhood, park and elevated city. The route
        supports those changes without requiring a generic Andes-backdrop description.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Figure
      src={`${IMG}/santiago-park-street.jpg`}
      srcset={`${IMG}/santiago-park-street-480w.jpg 480w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-street-768w.jpg 768w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-street-1200w.jpg 1200w, ${IMG}/santiago-park-street.jpg 1365w`}
      sizes={SIZES_MODERN_WIDE}
      alt="A tree-lined Santiago park setting; exact location is unresolved."
      width={1365}
      height={2048}
      caption="A quieter Santiago street and park register; exact location unresolved."
      variant="portrait"
    />

    <SectionHeading id="omitted">What I Have Left Out</SectionHeading>
    <div class="narrative prose">
      <p>
        Valparaíso remains excluded because “Valparaiso??” is a planning note and the
        route does not record a completed day trip. The Bahá’í Temple stays out because
        its viewpoint-specific evidence is too weak for this hub. Cementerio General
        is acknowledged as a recorded supporting stop, not expanded into a cemetery
        article without stronger first-hand detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        I have also left out food claims, market purchases, running, cable-car or
        funicular assumptions, current prices, opening hours, queues, safety, weather
        and transport fares. Those details may be useful in a live planning guide, but
        they are not established by this first-hand record.
      </p>
    </div>

    <FurtherReading
      label="Continue through Santiago"
      cards={[
        { href: '/4-days-in-santiago-itinerary/', region: 'Chile · Santiago', title: '4 Days in Santiago: Complete Itinerary', excerpt: 'The full chronology through civic Santiago, Vega Central, museums, parks, Yungay, the viewpoints and the bus to Mendoza.', meta: ['Itinerary', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/santiago-viewpoints/', region: 'Chile · Santiago', title: 'Santiago Viewpoints: San Cristóbal, Santa Lucía and Sky Costanera', excerpt: 'A focused comparison of the three recorded ways to read Santiago from above, with access and image limits kept visible.', meta: ['Viewpoints', 'September 2026'] },
        { href: '/24-days-in-south-america-itinerary/', region: 'South America · Chile', title: '24 Days in South America', excerpt: 'The wider route from La Paz through Santiago and Mendoza before the journey turns east.', meta: ['Pillar itinerary', '2026'] },
      ]}
    />
  </div>
</ChronicleLayout>
